**Definition 6.1 (Attribution and domains of the ingredients).** The
preparation-stabilizer normal form, the specialized full-block
criterion (3.5), and the full-TP separation (4.7) are repository-derived
consequences of the explicit QRC basis. General ingredients are
credited within those proofs. In particular the invariant-product
convex-hull method is established in
[Vollbrecht–Werner, §II.2](https://arxiv.org/html/quant-ph/0010095v1);
its use here keeps the actual joint group $\mathcal G$ and
independent product factors. The binary square-root inequality has
the same two-coordinate mechanism as
[Singh–Nechita, Proposition 6.7](https://arxiv.org/html/2010.07898v2).
Their diagonal-orthogonal covariance classes are not substituted
for the present walk group. The principal-minor criterion and
finite Fourier quadrature are mature linear-algebra ingredients,
not new standalone results of this source.

The representation-dependent averaging in
[Nechita–Park, §2.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2403.03667v2)
does not license full $O(5)$ menu invariance. The low-dimensional
separability spaces in
[Hildebrand, Theorems 3.8, 3.9 and 8.1](https://arxiv.org/html/math/0701571v1)
do not make this $5\times5$ Choi cone equal to PPT: the $3+2$
split in (3.8) is a direct sum in a measurement quadratic form,
not a two-by-something tensor factorization of the Choi state.
The distinction between separable sums and per-factor normalization
in [Ohst et al., Remark 10](https://arxiv.org/html/2411.08110v2)
also persists: (5.5) normalizes the common sum and does not impose
TP on each individual generator. No literature assertion cited
here evaluates this actual-menu minimax. Repository-derived does
not assert worldwide novelty.

The first-read identity, no-worse stationarity and real replacement,
the exact cone and marginal, its affine dimension, and QJM's slack
identity remain credited supplier results. Definitions 1.1–1.5
and (5.4) specify their use; their restatement is not additional
mathematical progress claimed for this source.

